# Spec §4.1 (several schedules), §4.4, §5.5, §5.6 and §5.7. Status of a task
# with more than one window per day.
#   - Calendar (§5.6): an occurrence is (occurrence day, window) and is
#     identified by its start. P = the greatest ocurrenciaCubierta; one with a time
#     is an instant; values without a time (a date alone, the day of completadoEn
#     without ocurrenciaCubierta, a day before creadaEn) cover the whole day.
#     N = the first occurrence starting after P; A = start of N and
#     C = the to of its window.
#   - One-off (§5.7): A = first instant >= creadaEn inside some window;
#     between windows, waiting until the from of the next one. With a due
#     date, being overdue is the to of the last window of the due date.
#   - Relative in days (§5.7): once per window on the days it is due;
#     never completed, all windows from the first whose to is
#     after creadaEn; with P, the round of the day of P continues and then
#     every `cada` days. Becoming overdue is C (not V + interval).
# Progress is a fraction between 0 and 1, compared exactly.
# Reference week: Monday 2026-07-13, Wednesday 15, Thursday 16; previous
# Thursday 2026-07-09.
Feature: Status with several schedules

  # Status with several schedules 1
  # Calendar: after completing the morning window, waiting
  # until the from of the afternoon one, with progress since the completion.
  Scenario Outline: Status with several schedules 1
    Given a weekly recurring task on <days>
    And the task has a completion from <completed> that covers <covered>
    And the task can be completed from <from> to <to> and from <from2> to <to2>
    When I query the status on <now>
    Then the status is en espera
    And the progress is <progress>

    Examples:
      | days              | completed           | covered             | from  | to    | from2  | to2    | now                 | progress |
      | miércoles, jueves | 2026-07-15T08:00:00 | 2026-07-15T07:00:00 | 07:00 | 10:00 | 18:00  | 21:00  | 2026-07-15T13:00:00 | 0.5      |

